Write a small recursive function that merges dicts level by level instead of just overwriting.

code:
def deep_merge(a, b):
    out = dict(a)
    for k, v in b.items():
        if k in out and isinstance(out[k], dict) and isinstance(v, dict):
            out[k] = deep_merge(out[k], v)
        else:
            out[k] = v
    return out

print(deep_merge({'a': {'x': 1}}, {'a': {'y': 2}}))  # {'a': {'x': 1, 'y': 2}}

real example:
Merging layered configs without losing nested defaults.

